enus from a bunch of the top  restaurants in palo alto on january 12  2013 they went live with the website  they got their first order for pad thai  within an hour and they just kept coming  there were two more on launch day five  the day after then seven then ten the  guys immediately saw that there was  demand for what they were doing but that  demand created a puzzle for them to  solve how would they take orders how  would they communicate those orders to  the restaurant and how would they track  the progress of deliveries tony set up a  google voice number for the team so any  incoming calls would go to all of their  phones at the same time they'd then ring  the restaurant and place the exact same  order drive over pick up the food and  take it to the customer once they  started bringing more drivers on board  they'd use the find my friends feature  on their iphones to track the deliveries  that first iteration was pretty janky  but they were tackling a genuinely hard  coordination problem food delivery is  harder than ride sharing because it's a  three-sided equation with ride sharing  you've got customers and drivers one  source of demand and one source of  supply to meet that demand there are  still tricky optimization problems but  it's kind of a basic model restaurants  introduce a second source of supply into  this equation orders can't be fulfilled  without the input of both the driver and  the restaurant you've either got a  driver with no payload or an order  getting cold at the restaurant as tony  put it you need to nail 15 small things  to get one delivery right except for  pizza places food delivery was rare  before companies like doordash came  along that's because restaurants had to  create and maintain their own delivery  operations they had to hire and pay the  drivers they had to track the progress  of deliveries and make sure the whole  system ran smoothly basically they had  to solve both sides of the supply  equation themselves only a few  restaurants could take on that type of  complexity most understandably didn't  even try which is why what palo alto  delivery was doing was genuinely  innovative and hard the team saw that  they had an idea which resonated with  restaurants and customers local  restaurants liked that their food became  visible to a bigger group of customers  customers loved it because they didn't  have to leave their apartment to get the  best pad thai wings or hummus in town it  was just the beginning but the  opportunities were getting bigger every  day so the guys said to each other let's  do this for real 100  but they needed to learn more about how  delivery actually worked so tony and the  rest of the team actually signed up to  be domino's drivers for a few weeks they  even drove for fedex for a while this is  more than an amusing little anecdote  it's a great example of the approach  which has typified the company's journey  an obsession with operating at the  lowest level of detail for doordash's  founders operating at the lowest level  of detail meant learning about the nuts  and bolts of delivery years before they  became a billion dollar company learning  how delivery actually worked meant they  could apply it to their business that  meant reducing costs passing savings  onto restaurants and customers and  outsmarting their competitors fully  committed they applied to y combinator  and were accepted into the 2013 summer  batch for tony evans stanley and andy  this was a signal that they were on the  right path but they'd only taken the  first steps first things first they  ditched the name palo alto delivery  because they didn't want it restricting  their addressable market the team used  three criteria to come up with their new  name it had to be easy to spell ideally  it would have two syllables or less and  the domain name needed to be available  there's that commitment to operating at  the lowest level of detail with this  little algorithm in mind they came up  with the name doordash no more palo alto  delivery then they cleaned up the  website they ditched the pdf menus and  got proper restaurant pages getting the  small details right is important but  without the right strategy it doesn't  really matter the team was about to  confront their two biggest choices by  far looking back they might have been  the two most important calls they ever  made how they handled them tells us a  lot about the kind of company these guys  were building y combinator culminates in  demo day where you pitch to a huge group  of investors and the summer 2013 cohort  was extremely competitive in fact when  techcrunch published an article covering  the top eight most promising companies  in the batch they didn't even mention  doordash the doordash team had come up  with a pitch that would stand out from  their competitors this was it the moment  that would determine if doordash could  make it to the next stage and secure a  solid funding round after demo day they  needed cash to scale up their operations  so they had to nail their pitch when  tony and the team presented doordash  they made a bold claim they weren't a  food delivery company they were building  a logistics business food delivery was  just the first use case it was audacious  but they knew that they could back it up  companies like grubhub and seamless  created demand for restaurants that  already managed and operated their own  delivery infrastructure doordash's pitch  was that in exchange for a share of  revenue they would build that  infrastructure they would take on the  risk of managing a workforce and  optimizing delivery routes doordash was  helping restaurants solve the delivery  problem by stepping in to be that second  half of the supply equation it was a  totally different approach to what their  rivals were doing this might mean  sacrificing early market share and  interest from certain investors but it  was a model that doordash could apply to  industries beyond food and this is that  lowest level of detail doordash raised  2.4 million dollars in seed funding out  of yc it wasn't a huge raise even then  companies were coming out of the gates  raising 5 million or more but it was  enough with that money in the bank the  founders had to make the second of two  huge early decisions where to expand  they started in palo alto where do you  think they went next san diego la maybe  outside of california how about san jose  specifically east san jose i want to put  this into context for people who might  not understand how unorthodox this was  san jose is a big city in the bay area  but east san jose is the suburbs  families who own homes and walk their  dogs down leafy streets that kind of  thing it couldn't possibly have the  density that a company like doordash  needed to turn a profit right wrong  because once again doordash saw  something others didn't first the  founders realized that the higher rates  of car ownership in the suburbs would  mean a greater supply of drivers but  their more important insight was that  expanding into the suburbs would allow  doordash to refine their operation and  introduce their business to customers  without much competition and here we are  again operating at the lowest level of  detail to get an edge this first move  beyond palo alto went well enough that  in may 2014 doordash raised 17.3 million  dollars in funding from sequoia capital  just 15 months after the guys were  driving around making deliveries  themselves they were now in charge of a  series a company valued at 73.5 million  dollars there's this great photo that  stanley took from right around then he's  holding his computer science homework  his tax forms a speeding ticket and the  sequoia term sheet it was a crazy time  and they were just getting started that  series a funded doordash's expansion to  los angeles and the team ran the same  playbook there lock down the suburbs  first the first location outside of  california was boston that choice was a  really interesting signal about how  doordash was thinking about the  challenges of expanding into big cities  boston is big but it's also very flat so  it's great for cycling those conditions  presented an opportunity to experiment  with dashers on bikes who could weave  through heavy traffic better than if  they were in a car again they were doing  things differently and finding small  ways to outsmart the competition in  march of 2015 a 40 million series b  round led by kleiner perkins valued  doordash at 600 million dollars doordash  was operating in eight markets but by  the end of the year they were active in  22 including two in canada interestingly  at this stage all of the 65 employees  even the co-founders were actually  required to deliver one day per month so  if you were near the stanford campus  around then you may have had your order  delivered by tony stanley or andy that  close proximity to the actual operation  of the business meant that the team saw  things that others couldn't they saw  that dashers needed access to dedicated  elevators their own parking spots and  separate serving stations at restaurants  the doordash team was deep in the weeds  and finding tons of small improvements  that would add up to a big competitive  edge so far so good but things were  about to become a whole lot more  complicated doordash was getting a lot  of publicity and it wasn't all good  publicity by 2015 several restaurants  were claiming that doordash had uploaded  menus to its website without permission  which meant that sometimes prices were  different on the doordash website than  at the restaurant and like many gig  economy companies at the time doordash  had to grapple with whether or not their  delivery drivers should be classified as  independent contractors or full-time  employees moving fast in a new industry  led to tons of tough decisions and  doordash was facing a ton of competition  as well grubhub still had by far the  biggest share of food delivery in the  u.s and uber which was flush with  investor cash launched ubereats often  the best thing to do when you're under  pressure is to grow so fast you can't be  caught unfortunately just as that  pressure ramped up doordash stumbled  sequoia was encouraging the team to  raise a series c round and was prepared  to commit 40 million dollars as long as  another firm was leading the round so  tony went out looking for a new vc firm  to lead doordash's series c he started  looking and he kept looking eventually  after six months it's now march 2016  doordash closed a 127 million dollar  series c round that valued the company  at 700 million dollars just 100 million  dollars more than the series b round  2016 was a rough year for the company  the us economy grew by just 1.6 percent  which spooked markets and slowed  investment doordash added only six new  markets across the whole year that  underwhelming funding round meant that  it needed to be careful with its cash  customer acquisition was still expensive  and their other costs weren't really  coming down its market share had grown  to 10 but that paled in comparison to  grubhub 70 and uber was burning through  money trying to enter the same on-demand  delivery market that combination of  slowing growth high costs low margins  and ambitious rivals has killed off lots  of promising companies it could have  done the same to doordash in fact it  nearly did in late 2017 the company  raised a 60 million loan we only learned  about this three years later when  doordash went public and we got to see  their s1 which is the sec document  companies file pre-ipo to this day we  don't know for sure which investors  provided the loan doordash may have been  weeks away from running out of cash and  yet by august 2018 it was valued at 4  billion  how do you explain this incredible  turnaround from near death to an almost  6x increase in value success has many  ingredients but there are three things  that stand out first is that in 2017  doordash began turning a profit on a  per-order basis this didn't mean that  the company was suddenly profitable  overall but at a time when common wisdom  said it was impossible to run a delivery  company without burning through piles of  vc money it was vital to demonstrate to  investors that you were making progress  towards that goal the second reason is  that doordash's existing investors kept  the faith companies like sequoia were  smart enough to know that doordash's  business model and commitment to  operating at the lowest level of detail  was enough to beat the competition  provided they survived the tough times  their faith was rewarded sequoia and  other long-time investors held big  stakes in doordash when it began  dominating the industry the other reason  can be boiled down to basically just one  thing softbank the softbank vision fund  was placing big bets on delivery and  ride sharing around this time this  included being one of the biggest  investors in uber softbank led  doordash's 535 million dollar series d  round in march of 2018. doordash was now  a unicorn but a highly deluded one  softbank and other investors had just  taken a 38 slice of the company it was a  high price to pay but it gave doordash  the cash it needed to hit the  accelerator and that's exactly what they  did in august they raised another 250  million dollars at less than five  percent dilution when they weren't even  actively looking for more investment  that round valued doordash had four  billion dollars which is a huge  valuation but deliveries grew by 250  over the course of 2018 so clearly  things were going well by the end of the  year the company was operating in more  than 3 000 markets thanks partly to big  chains like chipotle and ihop signing up  that massive increase to their footprint  enabled doordash to overtake grubhub to  become the number one food delivery  company in north america by march 2019.  from the verge of extinction doordash  was going stratospheric in may doordash  announced another massive funding round  which valued the company at 12.6 billion  dollars this valuation came on the back  of 60 growth since its series f  expansion to 4 000 markets in north  america and its dash pass service  approaching a million subscribers and in  2019 doordash made 880 million dollars  in revenue off of 8 billion in gross  order value tellingly the company  extracted 60 more revenue in 2019 from  the same restaurants that were on its  platform the previous year its growth  wasn't just broad it was also deep so  nothing could stop it right sometimes  companies generate enough momentum that  only two things can change their  trajectory a black swan event or an  unforced air in 2019 a new york times  piece revealed that rather than going  straight to dashers customer tips were  only going to them if they otherwise  wouldn't hit a guaranteed minimum take  in practice it meant that millions of  tips were going straight to doordash not  dashers it was a public relations  nightmare delivery drivers work super  hard for what is often low pay without  benefits strategically it was also a  risk wasn't doordash kind of admitting  that it needed this tipping scheme to  make money it quickly reversed the  policy but that was too late to assuage  people that thought that doordash was  too eager to move fast and break things  and investors started to get concerned  that doordash's unit economics wouldn't  hold strong without the tips the issue  of worker classification is extremely  complex but before that could be  resolved there was the small matter of a  global pandemic beyond its human costs  covid19 destroyed incumbent business  models disrupted supply chains and  accelerated existing social and economic  trends it was terrible news for  businesses that relied on foot traffic  but it was absolutely incredible for  doordash in march of 2020 the company  grew by more than 20 percent and they  were already massive at this point from  being nervous about the entry of  ubereats into the market and the head  start that they were giving up to  incumbents like grubhub doordash quickly  captured half of the u.s market in june  it raised another 400 million dollars in  funding at a 16 billion valuation but  november 3rd brought a prize that was  arguably even bigger 59 of voters in  california voted yes on proposition 22  which meant that rideshare and delivery  companies could classify their workers  as independent contractors rather than  employees entitled to benefits gig  economy companies had always argued that  classifying their workers as employees  would force them to raise prices and  dordash clearly thought the outcome was  important to the viability of their  business model they spent almost 48  million out of a total of 200 million to  support proposition 22. that argument  isn't over though in august last year a  judge overturned the law created by the  passage of proposition 22 and gig  economy companies are appealing the  ruling a very similar proposition will  be on the ballots in massachusetts this  november and it's going to be an  expensive fight but doordash is such a  large company now that worker  classification issues in a few states  aren't really an existential threat  anymore after all doordash was now big  enough to start planning an ipo and the  combination of the pandemic  turbocharging demand for food delivery  the outcome of proposition 22 and a  buoyant market for tech stocks gave it  the perfect opportunity the ipo priced  doordash at 102 per share by the end of  the first day of trading the shares were  now valued at 189  an 86 increase for the day in a season  of absolutely crazy tech listings  doordashes was one of the most  ridiculous and the stock price kept  climbing now obviously doordash hasn't  been immune to the broader downturn in  tech stocks there's a ton of factors  affecting the public markets right now  and increasing volatility but there are  other numbers that tell us much more  about why this company has emerged as  the market leader in food delivery in  the united states it's still not  profitable but it is reliably generating  positive operating cash flows investors  aren't watching the profit numbers  though they're looking at revenue growth  and doordash's revenue has exploded 1.3  billion dollars in q4 of 2021 compared  to less than 300 million dollars just  two years earlier total orders increased  from 83 million in all of 2018 to 369  million in just the last three months of  2021 and they've done a great job  increasing customer loyalty nearly  quadrupling the amount of money each  doordash customer spends on average the  company's huge market reach means its  marginal marketing and customer  acquisition costs are decreasing this is  what happens when you get the flywheel  spinning doordash's latest earnings  report put its gross order value at 42  billion dollars in 2021 but compare that  to amazon which generated a staggering  600 billion dollars of gross order value  in the same time frame and you realize  that despite being a big fish doordash  is swimming in a medium-sized pond the  size of its addressable market is the  biggest constraint on its growth the  doordash team knows this though in fact  they've been preparing to overcome this  constraint for years remember what tony  and his co-founders told yc at demo day  back in 2013 we're not a food delivery  company we're building a logistics  business the platform that doordash  built helped restaurants deliver food to  their customers but food was only ever  the first use case since then doordash  has diversified its product offerings to  consumers and merchants let's start with  dashpass launched in 2018 dashpass is a  membership program where subscribers pay  9.99 a month for free delivery from  qualifying partner merchants it's the  same basic concept as amazon prime and  allows them to extract higher revenues  from the most dedicated customers the  average order from a subscriber does  generate lower contribution margins but  these members have higher retention and  higher order frequency which drives  faster growth in the last quarter of  2021 this dash pass program surpassed 10  million subscribers then there's  doordash for work which launched in  october 2020 and enables employers to  feed their staff while they're working  from home this allows employees to make  subsidized doordash orders employers get  to keep their staff at their desks for  longer and doordash opened a new revenue  stream and that's not all doordash now  has three new products aimed squarely at  merchants doordash drive launched in  2016 is a white label last mile  fulfillment service that allows  businesses to deliver orders that  originated from outside the doordash  ecosystem effectively piggybacking off  the large pool of dashers and the  company's logistics operation storefront  launched in mid-2020 enables merchants  to manage online orders by creating a  simple website this is valuable because  doordash estimates that 40 of its  restaurants don't have any kind of  online ordering system and its most  radical product offering is its most  recent in early february 2022 doordash  announced doordash capital a program to  offer merchants cash advances that they  pay back automatically with doordash  sales doordash calls this a cash advance  not alone merchants are charged a  one-time fixed fee instead of a typical  interest structure and the amount a  merchant will be eligible to borrow will  be based on its past sales history with  doordash doordash capital is quite  different from past product offerings  which have fundamentally been consumer  loyalty programs or initiatives to  encourage merchants to use the company's  logistics operation nevertheless this  shows that along with its sustained  growth in non-restaurant orders doordash  has its eyes set on bigger markets than  just food delivery since it 